  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 203 AM CDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery currently shows one upper impulse digging\nacross the Pacific Northwest with another piece of energy moving\ninto Central CA. Just like last night a large MCS is tracking \nacross Nebraska with outflow surging south from the storms.\nOutflow will continue to push south this morning and by sunrise\nwill extend from just north of KRSL down to near KGCK. Boundary\nwill remain far enough north to allow for another day of extreme\nheat area wide, with temps very close to where they were \nyesterday. Models have been fairly consistent in developing a \nfew showers/storms near the front late this afternoon with \nactivity becoming more numerous over west-central KS as some \nupper energy lifts out of NM and across western KS. Some \nscattered showers and storms will linger tonight from northwest \nKS through eastern NE, possibly skirting our northwest fringes. \nThere will be more uncertainty with temps on Fri compared to \nthe last few days, as there are a few model solutions pushing \nthe boundary through central KS. Will go with the thinking that \nit will make it south of I-70 with a chance a few locations in \ncentral KS may stay below 100. \n\nAnother round of storms will be likely Fri night from northwest\nKS through Nebraska as some additional monsoonal energy lift out\nacross the Central Plains. Will go ahead and keep the front\nnorth of the area for Saturday, which will result in another day\nof extreme heat. The good news is that there is good model\nconsistency in a more substantial piece of energy lifting out \nof the Rockies and across the Northern/Central Plains and will \nfinally push the cold front through most of the area for Sunday,\nknocking highs just below 100 for all but southeast and far \nsouthern KS. As cooler air continues to spill south, there is a \nchance much of central KS may see highs stay in the 80s for \nMonday. At this time, it appears our best rain chances will be \nfor Sun night, especially across western portions of our \nforecast area. Confidence is very low beyond the Tue time frame,\nas there are significant differences with the med range models.\nGFS tries to dig some shortwave energy over the Pacific \nNorthwest into the Great Basin, while the ECMWF builds a strong \nridge across the Rockies through the Southern Plains. \nUnfortunately, the ECMWF does have some support from the UKMET \nand Canadian.",
